.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Wed Sep 30 2026
(This evening through Thursday night)

Mostly clear skies predominate with limited patches of stratus along
the Big Sur Coast. Tonight the stratus deck will come into the North
Bay valleys, the SF and Monterey Bay regions, and the northern
Salinas Valley, with expansion into the interior not expected
tonight. Today marks the first day of warm temperatures as a ridge
sets up over the Western United States, with highs ranging from the
middle 80s to middle 90s in the inland valleys, up to the upper 90s
for the warmest spots in the interior Central Coast, the middle 70s
to middle 80s along the Bays, and the middle 60s to the lower 70s
along the Pacific coast. Highs will be very similar on Thursday.
Winds will be relatively light with an onshore breeze developing in
the afternoon and evening hours, and a push of offshore wind in the
higher elevations of the North and East Bay tonight through Thursday
morning. This morning gusts of 48 mph were reported on Mount St.
Helena and 46 mph at the Santa Fe Geothermal station within the
Mayacamas, with other exposed ridges in the interior North and East
Bays reporting peak gusts in the 25-35 mph range. Expect similar
gusts tonight, resulting in poor overnight humidity recoveries and
elevated fire weather conditions.

Areas of Moderate HeatRisk are starting to pop up across the inland
regions, and expand across the inland regions of the East Bay, South
Bay, and the Central Coast on Thursday. These correspond to a
moderate risk of heat-related illnesses for sensitive populations,
including children, the elderly, pregnant women, people with chronic
illnesses, and people who work or live outdoors without access to
adequate cooling or hydration. Follow these tips to avoid heat
exhaustion and heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

Minor coastal flooding is possible across the bayshore locations of
San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay around high tide.
Up to 1.2 feet of inundation above normally dry land is expected,
including up to 0.7 ft of surge caused by thermal expansion and sea
level rise since the sea level datum (basically the point where we
define as "sea level") was set in the 1983-2001 period. At the San
Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.2 ft above
normal (7.03 ft MLLW) at 2:10 PM Wednesday 9/30 and 1.1 ft above
normal (6.92 ft MLLW) at 2:58 PM on Thursday 10/1.

