.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Wed Sep 30 2026
(Friday through next Tuesday)

The ridging remains rather resilient through the seven-day outlook
and temperatures remain warmer than the seasonal average through the
week. A notable warming trend will take place over the weekend with
temperatures warming to the 90s into the triple digits (as warm as
102 degrees in the warmest spots of interior Monterey and San benito
Counties). Moderate HeatRisk will become widespread across the
inland locations, and there is a decent chance (40-60% probability)
that areas within the interior East Bay, South Bay, Santa Lucia
range, and interior Central Coast reach Major HeatRisk. That would
correspond to a more widespread risk for heat-related illnesses for
most people, and if this scenario remains on track through the next
day, Heat Advisories for these areas are likely. Longer-range
outlooks suggest that temperatures above seasonal averages are very
likely (70-90% probability) into the second week of October, and
this is supported by ensemble model cluster analysis, where all
clusters agree on keeping some form of ridging around across the
first week of October.

